<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Katō Kiyomasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1562">1562</a>–<a href="/years/1611">1611</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Katō Kiyomasa was a Japanese general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> who received northern Higo in 1588 and became a leading commander in the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Siding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600, he gained all of Higo and built the great castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a>. His son lost the domain in 1632, and it passed to the Hosokawa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kato-kiyomasa.jpg" alt="Katō Kiyomasa" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kat%C5%8D_Kiyomasa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Katō Kiyomasa was born in what is now Nakamura-ku, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a>, to Katō Kiyotada, whose wife Ito was a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>'s mother. His father died while he was young, and he soon entered Hideyoshi's service, receiving a stipend of 170 koku in 1576 at the age of 15. He fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamazaki">Battle of Yamazaki</a> in 1582 and at Shizugatake in 1583, where his achievements made him one of Hideyoshi's Seven Spears of Shizugatake and brought 3,000 more koku. In 1584 he fought at Komaki and Nagakute against the Tokugawa. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a> campaign of 1587 he was granted 250,000 koku in Higo, roughly half the province, with Kumamoto Castle as his residence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kiyomasa was one of three senior commanders in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imjin-war">Imjin War</a> against Joseon Korea (1592–1598). Together with Konishi Yukinaga he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>, Busan and many other cities, defeated the last Korean regulars at the Battle of the Imjin River and pacified Hamgyong. He held two Korean princes hostage to force lower officials to surrender. A noted castle-builder, he built strategic Japanese-style castles in the lands he took, among them Ulsan, whose siege, begun on 22 December 1597, was his best-known battle: he defended it against a Chinese army under Yang Hao said to number 60,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a> did not report his bravery to Hideyoshi. Like other daimyō he brought captive Korean potters back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a>.</p><p>After Hideyoshi's death in 1598 a group of seven generals including Kiyomasa turned against Mitsunari over his reports of the Korean war, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> mediated. Kiyomasa then sided with Ieyasu, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> campaign of 1600 he fought Mitsunari's allies on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a> and took a number of castles. As a reward he received the other half of Higo, bringing his income to nearly 500,000 koku.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his later years Kiyomasa tried to mediate between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> and Toyotomi Hideyori. In 1611, on a sea voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a> after one such meeting, he fell ill and died shortly after he arrived. He was buried at Honmyō-ji in Kumamoto and is enshrined at many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> shrines, among them Katō Shrine in Kumamoto. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-tadahiro">Katō Tadahiro</a> succeeded him, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemitsu">Tokugawa Iemitsu</a> confiscated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> and exiled him in 1632 on suspicion of conspiracy. A devoted follower of Nichiren Buddhism, Kiyomasa encouraged Nichiren temples in his lands. He is noted for brutally suppressing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and clashed with Konishi Yukinaga, who held the other half of Higo and was a Christian. In 1910 he was posthumously promoted to junior third court rank.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Katō Kiyomasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Kato Kiyomasa</b>, one of Toyotomi Hideyoshi's leading generals, received northern Higo in 1588 and the whole province after supporting Tokugawa Ieyasu in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/state/kumamoto-domain">Kumamoto Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Myōgyō-ji Temple, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.17,136.86&amp;year=1593" aria-label="Myōgyō-ji Temple on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8016 892 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Katō Kiyomasa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8466 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8466 1162h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.17,136.86&amp;year=1593">What was here in 1593? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
